From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz Sobala <lsobala1@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 02:42:56 +0200
Subject: riscv: dts: sophgo: disable the unused Milk-V Duo S coprocessor

The board DTS enables remoteproc for the companion C906L core, and the SoC DTSI
reserves 2MiB at the top of RAM for its firmware. Nothing uses either: the
bootloader deliberately builds no FreeRTOS image for that core (documented in
config/sources/families/include/sophgo-sg200x_common.inc), and no cvirtos.elf
is installed for remoteproc to load, so the core never starts.

Turn the node off and release the region. On a 512MiB board the 2MiB is worth
having back, and reserved-memory regions are honoured regardless of whether their
consumer is enabled, so disabling the node alone would have changed nothing -
memory-region has to go and the region with it.

The region is deleted by label. Its node name differs between the two
architectures - region@8fe00000 on riscv64, from the upstream patch that added it
with a name that disagrees with its own reg, and region@9fe00000 on arm64 from
0039 - and /delete-node/ against a name that is not there is silently accepted,
which is a good way to ship a patch that does nothing. Checked by decompiling
both DTBs and confirming no region remains.

This is the default, not a verdict. The c906l overlay added later in the series
restores the node and the region at boot, so anyone with firmware to run gets the
coprocessor back without editing the board DTS.

Signed-off-by: Armbian <info@armbian.com>
---
 arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts | 27 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
@@ -348,6 +348,31 @@ &int_adc {
 	/delete-property/ mute-gpio-r;
 };
 
+/*
+ * The SG2000 has a second, much smaller RISC-V core (a C906L) alongside the one
+ * running Linux. It is meant for real-time work with no operating system in the
+ * way - Milk-V's "Arduino on the Duo" runs sketches there, on FreeRTOS - and
+ * remoteproc is how Linux would load firmware onto it and start it.
+ *
+ * Nothing here uses it by default. Our bootloader deliberately ships no FreeRTOS
+ * image (see sophgo-sg200x_common.inc), we install no cvirtos.elf for remoteproc
+ * to load, and the core stays parked. What it did cost is the 2MiB at the top of
+ * RAM that the reserved-memory region holds for it, on a board with 512MiB total
+ * - and that region is reserved whether or not this node is enabled, so
+ * disabling the node alone would not give the memory back. Hence both.
+ *
+ * Deleting by label rather than by node name on purpose: the region is
+ * region@8fe00000 on riscv64 and region@9fe00000 on arm64, and /delete-node/ on a
+ * name that does not exist is silently accepted.
+ *
+ * None of this is permanent, and undoing it does not mean editing this file: the
+ * c906l overlay puts the node and the region back at boot. Set overlays=c906l in
+ * /boot/armbianEnv.txt and supply /lib/firmware/cvirtos.elf. See
+ * overlay/sg2000-c906l.dtso.
+ */
 &remoteproc {
-	status = "okay";
+	/delete-property/ memory-region;
+	status = "disabled";
 };
+
+/delete-node/ &coprocessor_rtos;
-- 
Armbian

